{"type":"command_run","dispatch_id":"01KQBBNJ223CRWTGBXX62QBMP9","ts":"2026-04-29T00:54:47Z","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '130,205p' skills/mission/SKILL.md\""}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' openspec/changes/deterministic-planning/design.md\"","aggregated_output":"## Context\n\nThe mission skill (`skills/mission/SKILL.md`, 837 lines) is the orchestrator's operating manual. It defines 10 phases (0-9) with gates between them. Templates in `skills/mission/templates/` define artifact formats. Prompts in `skills/mission/prompts/` are handed to validator subagents. The openspec config at `openspec/config.yaml` injects project-level context and rules into artifact generation.\n\nThis change adds deterministic constraints to the planning phases (4.3, 4.4, 5, 8) without restructuring the existing phase flow. All changes are additive -- no existing phases are removed or reordered.\n\n## Goals / Non-Goals\n\n**Goals:**\n- Constrain the 5 highest-leverage decisions in spec generation: architecture choice, design specificity, task decomposition, convention establishment, and spec traceability\n- Reduce variance between runs against identical PRDs by pinning decisions through rubrics, templates, gates, and anchors\n- Keep changes compatible with the existing phase flow and OpenSpec schema\n\n**Non-Goals:**\n- Forking the OpenSpec schema (use config rules instead)\n- Modifying validator prompts (scrutiny, user-test, adversarial)\n- Modifying the worker brief template beyond what's needed for these features\n- Guaranteeing identical outputs (reducing variance, not eliminating it)\n- Enforcing ubiquitous language depth (tracked in issue #130)\n\n## Decisions\n\n### D1: Three-layer design enforcement (config rules + template + gate)\n\n**Choice:** Inject mandatory section requirements via `openspec/config.yaml` rules.design entries. Define section content formats in `templates/design-template.md`. Verify sections exist via a SKILL.md gate in Phase 4.3.\n\n**Alternative considered:** Fork the OpenSpec schema to modify the design.md template directly. Rejected because a fork creates sync burden with upstream OpenSpec changes. Config rules achieve the same injection without maintenance cost.\n\n**Alternative considered:** Put all content in SKILL.md Phase 4.3 instructions. Rejected because the rubrics and table formats are too detailed for inline SKILL.md text. The template file keeps SKILL.md lean.\n\n### D2: Architecture scoring lives in the design template, not SKILL.md\n\n**Choice:** The four-dimension rubric with per-level criteria is defined in `templates/design-template.md`. SKILL.md Phase 4.3 references it and runs the gate. The scoring table appears in design.md as the Architecture Decision section. The hard floor (no dimension below 1) and tie escalation (present tied candidates to human) are enforced by the SKILL.md gate in Phase 4.3, not by the template.\n\n**Why:** The rubric is ~40 lines of structured criteria tables. Inlining this in SKILL.md (already 837 lines) would bloat the orchestrator's main document. The template is read on demand when Phase 4.3 runs. The gate enforces the behavioral rules (floor, ties) because those are orchestrator decisions, not template content.\n\n### D3: Type Catalog has no field-level detail\n\n**Choice:** Types list name, purpose, and owning module. No fields.\n\n**Why:** Field definitions are implementation decisions derived from spec WHEN/THEN blocks. The v3/v5 divergence was about type *names* (IronTodoError vs DomainError), not type *fields*. The Type Catalog prevents naming drift; fields are the worker's job.\n\n### D4: Phase 4.3a inserted between design and tasks\n\n**Choice:** New sub-phase for task strategy approval. The orchestrator presents decomposition approach, target task count, and dependency rationale. The human approves before tasks.md is generated.\n\n**Why:** In the v3/v5 comparison, v3 used layered decomposition (7 groups) and v5 used flat vertical slices (15 tasks). Making this an explicit human decision prevents the model from silently choosing a strategy.\n\n**Insertion point:** After Phase 4.3 (design review), before Phase 4.4 (task generation). The Phase Index is updated to include 4.3a. Note: 5 of the 6 Deterministic Anchors are populated immediately after design approval in Phase 4.3. The task decomposition strategy anchor is populated after Phase 4.3a approval, since the strategy doesn't exist until the human approves it.\n\n### D5: Task topology as inline metadata, not a separate artifact\n\n**Choice:** Each task in tasks.md includes topology fields directly in the task description, using a fixed markdown format:\n\n```\n- [ ] Task N: <name>\n  Depends on: Task 1, Task 2\n  Consumes: Todo type (domain), store interface (storage)\n  Produces: search endpoint (transport), SearchResult type (domain)\n  Layers: domain, transport\n```\n\n**Why fixed format:** The foundation audit (D9) reads the Produces field to extract module and type names for its check. Loose formatting would make the audit brittle. The four fields use consistent casing and comma-separated values.\n\n**Layers are logical groupings** (domain, transport, storage, configuration, etc.) defined in the design narrative, not columns from the Module Inventory table. The Module Inventory lists modules; layers group modules by architectural concern.\n\n**Alternative considered:** Separate `topology.md` file. Rejected because it splits task information across two files and creates a sync burden. Inline metadata keeps everything in one place.\n\n### D6: Task quality standard updated to be strategy-aware\n\n**Choice:** Phase 4.4's task quality standard changes from \"each task must be a vertical slice\" to \"each task must produce a concrete, testable output.\" When the approved strategy (Phase 4.3a) is vertical, tasks cross all layers. When the strategy is layered or hybrid, tasks may be scoped to a single layer, provided each task still has a clear done state and a test surface.\n\n**Why:** The current rule hardcodes vertical slices as the only valid approach. In the v3/v5 benchmark, v3 used layered decomposition (ignoring the rule) and scored 12 points higher. The rule was already being overridden by model judgment. Making it strategy-aware aligns the rule with Phase 4.3a and eliminates the contradiction.\n\n**What stays:** The `/tdd` skill's vertical slice guidance is unchanged -- it describes how to implement within a task (test one path, make it pass, repeat), not how to scope tasks. A layered task still uses TDD vertical slices internally.\n\n**Cascade updates required:** This wording change must also be applied to `openspec/config.yaml` rules.tasks and the `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md` Vertical Slice definition to maintain consistency. The `docs/design-decisions.md` references are historical and stay as-is.\n\n### D7: Spec references in contract replace row numbers (renumbered from D6)\n\n**Choice:** Contract template changes from `<!-- PRD Requirements: #1, #3 -->` to `<!-- Specs: add-todo/1, search/3 -->`. Phase 5 instructions updated to match.\n\n**Why:** Row numbers are arbitrary and break if the coverage audit is reordered. Spec capability/scenario identifiers are stable and meaningful. The coverage audit already maps PRD to specs, so there's no new mapping to create.\n\n### D8: CLAUDE.md + ARCH.md as closing task, not pre-build phase\n\n**Choice:** Generate CLAUDE.md and ARCH.md as a mandatory closing task (between module shape re-check and documentation). Create only if absent. Template follows Claude Code best practices.\n\n**Alternative considered:** Generate CLAUDE.md before the build loop (Phase 4.5). Rejected because: (a) during the build, worker briefs already carry architecture excerpts, key terms, and codebase deltas; (b) for greenfield projects, task 1 creates the build system so commands don't exist yet; (c) generating from the actual built codebase produces a more accurate CLAUDE.md.\n\n**ARCH.md:** Extracted from design.md as a living document. CLAUDE.md's Rules section includes \"update ARCH.md when you change architecture\" so it stays current.\n\n### D9: Foundation audit scoped to task 1's declared produces\n\n**Choice:** After task 1 passes both validators, check module structure and type names against design.md, but only for what task 1 said it would produce (from its \"produces\" topology field).\n\n**Why:** Task 1 typically creates the project foundation but not all modules. Checking the full design would fail by definition since tasks 2-N haven't run. Scoping to declared produces avoids false positives while catching the naming and structure drift that cascaded in v5.\n\n### D10: Deterministic anchors as one-liner pointers\n\n**Choice:** Six fields in plan.md, each a one-line summary with a source reference. Not duplicated content from design.md.\n\n**Why:** The plan must stay concise to survive compaction effectively. Full content duplication would bloat the plan and create a sync problem. Pointers tell the orchestrator what was decided and where to find details if needed.\n\n## Risks / Trade-offs\n\n**[Risk] Architecture scoring adds time to planning.** Evaluating 2-3 candidates with a rubric takes longer than generating one architecture directly.\n→ Mitigation: For greenfield missions, the time invested prevents the cascade of problems seen in v5 (file size, complexity, naming drift). For existing codebases without architecture changes, scoring is skipped. For existing codebases with explicit architecture changes, the current architecture is included as one of the 2-3 candidates.\n\n**[Risk] Config rules are advisory, not enforced by OpenSpec.** The openspec CLI injects rules into instructions, but the LLM could still produce a design without all sections.\n→ Mitigation: The SKILL.md gate catches missing sections and regenerates. The config rules are guidance; the gate is enforcement.\n\n**[Risk] Task topology metadata adds review burden.** Each task now has 4 extra fields the human must review.\n→ Mitigation: The orchestrator generates the metadata -- the human reviews, not writes. The >2 layers flag focuses attention on problematic tasks. For small missions (2-3 tasks), the metadata is trivial.\n\n**[Risk] Foundation audit may trigger unnecessary corrective tasks.** After task 1, minor naming differences could be flagged as drift.\n→ Mitigation: Corrective tasks require human approval. The human can say \"this is fine\" and proceed. The audit is a signal, not an automatic fix.\n\n**[Risk] CLAUDE.md template may produce generic content.** Even with the include/exclude guidance, the closing task worker might produce boilerplate.\n→ Mitigation: The template has concrete sections (Commands, Repo Map, Testing, Environment) filled from the actual codebase. The include/exclude table explicitly bans generic content. The human reviews the closing task output through normal validation.\n\n## File Change Map\n\n| File | Change type | Lines added (est.) |\n|------|------------|-------------------|\n| `skills/mission/SKILL.md` | Modify phases 4.3, 4.4, 5, 8; add 4.3a; update Phase Index | ~75 |\n| `skills/mission/templates/design-template.md` | New file | ~70 |\n| `skills/mission/templates/claude-md.md` | New file | ~50 |\n| `skills/mission/templates/plan.md` | Add Deterministic Anchors section | ~12 |\n| `skills/mission/templates/contract.md` | Update spec reference format | ~2 |\n| `openspec/config.yaml` | Add rules.design entries; update rules.tasks vertical-slice wording to be strategy-aware | ~12 |\n| `README.md` | Reflect all changes | ~20 |\n| `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md` | Already updated in Phase 2 | 0 |\n\n## Open Questions\n\nNone remaining -- all decisions resolved during grill session.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"tool_end","dispatch_id":"01KQBBNJ223CRWTGBXX62QBMP9","ts":"2026-04-29T00:54:47Z","tool":"command_execution"}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' openspec/changes/deterministic-planning/specs/design-structure/spec.md\"","aggregated_output":"## ADDED Requirements\n\n### Requirement: Mandatory design sections\nDesign.md SHALL include five mandatory sections defined in the design template reference document.\n\n#### Scenario: Design generation follows template\n- **WHEN** the orchestrator generates design.md during Phase 4.3\n- **THEN** the design MUST include sections for: Architecture Decision, Module Inventory, Type Catalog, Error Strategy, and Integration Points\n\n#### Scenario: Architecture Decision section content\n- **WHEN** the Architecture Decision section is written (greenfield or arch-change mission)\n- **THEN** it MUST contain a scoring table with 2-3 candidates, their scores on all four dimensions, and the rationale for the chosen architecture\n\n#### Scenario: Module Inventory section content\n- **WHEN** the Module Inventory section is written\n- **THEN** it MUST contain a table with columns for module name, interface width, what it hides, and why this boundary exists\n\n#### Scenario: Type Catalog section content\n- **WHEN** the Type Catalog section is written\n- **THEN** it MUST list each domain type with name (from UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md), purpose (one line), and owning module -- without field-level detail\n\n#### Scenario: Error Strategy section content\n- **WHEN** the Error Strategy section is written\n- **THEN** it MUST specify the error type hierarchy, propagation rules, and which layer throws vs catches\n\n#### Scenario: Integration Points section content\n- **WHEN** the Integration Points section is written\n- **THEN** it MUST describe how modules call each other with caller, callee, method/interface, and direction\n\n### Requirement: OpenSpec config rules injection\nThe mandatory section requirements SHALL be injected into design generation instructions via openspec config.yaml rules.\n\n#### Scenario: Config rules present\n- **WHEN** openspec config.yaml contains rules.design entries listing the five mandatory sections\n- **THEN** the openspec CLI MUST include those rules in the instructions returned for design generation\n\n### Requirement: Design template reference document\nA reference document SHALL exist at `templates/design-template.md` defining the content format for each mandatory section, including the architecture scoring rubric with per-level criteria.\n\n#### Scenario: Template file exists\n- **WHEN** the mission skill is loaded\n- **THEN** `templates/design-template.md` MUST exist and define content formats for all five mandatory sections, including the four-dimension scoring rubric with concrete per-level criteria for each dimension\n\n### Requirement: Design section gate\nThe orchestrator SHALL verify all five mandatory sections exist before presenting design.md for human review. The gate also enforces the architecture scoring hard floor and tie escalation.\n\n#### Scenario: All sections present\n- **WHEN** design.md contains headings for all five mandatory sections\n- **THEN** the orchestrator proceeds to present the design for human review\n\n#### Scenario: Missing section detected\n- **WHEN** design.md is missing one or more of the five mandatory section headings\n- **THEN** the orchestrator MUST regenerate the design before presenting it to the human\n\n#### Scenario: Architecture candidate scores zero\n- **WHEN** the recommended architecture candidate scores 0 on any dimension\n- **THEN** the gate MUST reject the design and require the orchestrator to recommend a different candidate or regenerate\n\n#### Scenario: Architecture candidates tie\n- **WHEN** two or more architecture candidates have the same total score\n- **THEN** the gate MUST present both tied candidates to the human for discussion and selection rather than applying an arbitrary tie-breaker\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"tool_end","dispatch_id":"01KQBBNJ223CRWTGBXX62QBMP9","ts":"2026-04-29T00:54:47Z","tool":"command_execution"}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' openspec/changes/deterministic-planning/specs/architecture-scoring/spec.md\"","aggregated_output":"## ADDED Requirements\n\n### Requirement: Architecture candidate evaluation\nThe orchestrator SHALL evaluate 2-3 architecture candidates during Phase 4.3 for greenfield missions or missions with explicit architecture changes.\n\n#### Scenario: Greenfield mission reaches design phase\n- **WHEN** the mission is greenfield (no existing codebase) and Phase 4.3 begins\n- **THEN** the orchestrator MUST present 2-3 architecture candidates with a scoring table before writing design.md\n\n#### Scenario: Existing codebase without architecture change\n- **WHEN** the mission modifies an existing codebase and the PRD does not request an architecture change\n- **THEN** the orchestrator MUST document the current architecture and explain how planned changes fit within it, without scoring candidates\n\n#### Scenario: Explicit architecture change requested\n- **WHEN** the PRD explicitly requests an architecture change (e.g., \"migrate from monolith to workspace\")\n- **THEN** the orchestrator MUST evaluate 2-3 candidates including the current architecture as one option\n\n### Requirement: Four-dimension scoring rubric\nEach architecture candidate SHALL be scored on four dimensions using a 0-3 scale with concrete per-level criteria.\n\n#### Scenario: Separation of concerns scoring\n- **WHEN** scoring a candidate on separation of concerns\n- **THEN** the orchestrator MUST assign: 3 if each responsibility maps to exactly one unit with no mixing of domain/transport/storage/config; 2 if mostly isolated but 1-2 units mix concerns pragmatically; 1 if several units mix concerns; 0 if no clear separation\n\n#### Scenario: Testability scoring\n- **WHEN** scoring a candidate on testability\n- **THEN** the orchestrator MUST assign: 3 if each module testable in isolation with swappable dependencies; 2 if most modules testable but 1-2 require real external dependencies; 1 if tests require significant setup or multiple services; 0 if no practical way to test in isolation\n\n#### Scenario: Task independence scoring\n- **WHEN** scoring a candidate on task independence\n- **THEN** the orchestrator MUST assign: 3 if separately buildable or deployable units where a task can modify one without touching others; 2 if explicit interface boundaries within a single build unit with enforced contracts; 1 if directory/namespace separation only with no enforcement; 0 if flat structure with no meaningful boundaries\n\n#### Scenario: Module depth scoring\n- **WHEN** scoring a candidate on module depth\n- **THEN** the orchestrator MUST assign: 3 if modules expose small public API relative to implementation; 2 if reasonable ratio but 1-2 modules expose more surface than they hide; 1 if several modules are thin wrappers; 0 if no meaningful abstraction\n\n### Requirement: Hard floor enforcement\nNo recommended architecture candidate SHALL score 0 on any dimension.\n\n#### Scenario: Candidate scores zero on a dimension\n- **WHEN** a candidate scores 0 on any of the four dimensions\n- **THEN** the orchestrator MUST flag the candidate and recommend against it, explaining which dimension failed and why\n\n### Requirement: Tie escalation to human\nWhen architecture candidates tie on total score, the orchestrator SHALL present both to the human for discussion and selection.\n\n#### Scenario: Two candidates tie on total score\n- **WHEN** two or more candidates have the same total score (sum of all four dimensions)\n- **THEN** the orchestrator MUST present the tied candidates with their tradeoffs and let the human choose, rather than applying an arbitrary tie-breaker\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"tool_end","dispatch_id":"01KQBBNJ223CRWTGBXX62QBMP9","ts":"2026-04-29T00:54:47Z","tool":"command_execution"}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '130,205p' skills/mission/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"\nThe `spec-driven` schema generates four artifacts in dependency order: `proposal -> specs -> design -> tasks`. Generate each artifact one at a time, present it to the human for review, and get approval before generating the next.\n\n**Do NOT run `/opsx:propose` as a single pass.** Instead, orchestrate artifact creation using the openspec CLI commands directly:\n\n1. `openspec new change \"<name>\"` -- create the change directory\n2. `openspec status --change \"<name>\" --json` -- get artifact order and dependencies\n3. For each artifact in order:\n   a. `openspec instructions <artifact-id> --change \"<name>\" --json` -- get instructions for this artifact\n   b. Create the artifact file following the instructions and template\n   c. `openspec status --change \"<name>\" --json` -- re-run status to refresh the artifact list (newly unblocked artifacts and per-capability spec IDs appear only after their dependencies are written)\n\n### Review Pattern (used at every review point in planning)\n\nEvery artifact review follows this pattern:\n1. **AI presents** -- show the artifact content to the human in the chat\n2. **AI recommends** -- state whether it looks good, flag any concerns, suggest specific changes if needed\n3. **Human decides** -- approve, request changes, or defer to the AI recommendation\n\nThe human can always say \"looks good\" or \"go with your recommendation\" to keep momentum. If changes are requested, revise and re-present.\n\n### Phase 4.1: Generate and Review Proposal\n\nGenerate `proposal.md` (what and why). This establishes the capabilities that will become spec files.\n\nPresent it to the human with a recommendation. If approved, proceed to Phase 4.2.\n\n### Phase 4.2: Generate and Review Specs\n\nGenerate `specs/<capability>/spec.md` files (one per capability listed in the proposal). Each spec contains formal WHEN/THEN requirements using SHALL/MUST language.\n\nPresent all spec files to the human with a recommendation. Specs are the formal requirements that flow into tasks and the contract. Missing or vague specs are the primary cause of missed requirements in the final build.\n\nIf approved, proceed to Phase 4.3.\n\n### Phase 4.3: Generate and Review Design\n\nGenerate `design.md` (how -- architecture decisions, alternatives considered, risks). Reference the proposal for motivation and specs for requirements.\n\nDesign is required for every mission. The build loop and worker briefs depend on design.md existing -- skipping it would break downstream phases.\n\n#### Module Depth\n\nFor each major component or module in the design, consider its depth. A deep module has a small interface hiding significant implementation. A shallow module has a large interface with thin logic behind it. Prefer deep modules -- they are more testable at the boundary, easier for workers to implement in a single task, and easier for subsequent workers to use correctly.\n\nFor each module, describe:\n- **Interface** -- what callers see (public API, CLI flags, function signatures)\n- **What it hides** -- the complexity behind the interface that callers don't need to know about\n- **Why this boundary** -- what makes this the right place to draw the line\n\nIf a module has a large interface relative to its implementation, ask whether it should be split differently or whether some of its interface belongs in a higher-level module. A good test: can a worker implement this module in a single task and a validator test it at the boundary? If not, the module may be too shallow or too broad.\n\n#### Existing Codebases\n\nWhen the mission modifies an existing codebase (not greenfield), the design must evaluate the current code structure against the planned architecture. For each module the design touches, read the existing code and assess whether it aligns with the target design. If existing modules are shallow, tightly coupled, or conflict with the planned architecture, the design should call this out explicitly and recommend refactoring as part of the implementation. These refactoring decisions flow naturally into task ordering in Phase 4.4 -- refactoring tasks go before the feature tasks that depend on them.\n\nPresent it to the human with a recommendation. If approved, proceed to Phase 4.4.\n\n### Phase 4.4: Generate and Review Tasks\n\nGenerate `tasks.md` (ordered checklist of implementation tasks).\n\n#### Task Quality Standard\n\nEach task must be a **vertical slice**: a thin end-to-end path through all layers (schema, API, UI, tests) that delivers narrow but complete functionality.\n\n**One task = one worker = one commit = one validation cycle.**\n\nA task is good if you can demo it and verify it independently after it lands.\n\nWhen a task adds public surface area to a module (new exports, API endpoints, CLI flags), include the implementation behind that surface in the same task. Do not split a module's interface from its implementation across tasks -- the per-task validator judges depth from a single diff and will flag an interface without the work behind it.\n\n#### Mandatory Closing Tasks\n\nEvery mission's tasks.md MUST include these closing tasks after all feature tasks:\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
