{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_19","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,80p' openspec/config.yaml\"","aggregated_output":"schema: spec-driven\n\ncontext: |\n  Darkfactory is a lightweight multi-agent mission system built from Claude Code\n  primitives (skills, subagents, plan mode) and Codex CLI.\n\n  It orchestrates autonomous code generation using three roles:\n  - Orchestrator (Claude Code session in plan mode)\n  - Workers (Codex by default, configurable)\n  - Validators (Claude subagents)\n\n  The system is built as Claude Code skills, markdown templates, and bash scripts.\n  No custom IDE or app. See docs/design-decisions.md and UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md\n  for full architecture and terminology.\n\nrules:\n  design:\n    - design.md must include a section heading for Architecture Decision\n    - design.md must include a section heading for Module Inventory\n    - design.md must include a section heading for Type Catalog\n    - design.md must include a section heading for Error Strategy\n    - design.md must include a section heading for Integration Points\n  tasks:\n    - Each task should be a vertical slice -- a thin but complete path through all layers that is demoable and verifiable on its own\n    - One task = one worker = one commit = one validation cycle\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"tool_end","dispatch_id":"01KQBBNJ223CRWTGBXX62QBMP9","ts":"2026-04-29T00:55:40Z","tool":"command_execution"}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' skills/mission/templates/design-template.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Design Template\n\nUse this template for every `design.md`. Include all five mandatory sections below.\n\n## Architecture Decision\n\nFor greenfield missions or missions with explicit architecture changes, evaluate 2-3 architecture candidates. Score each candidate on every dimension from 0-3, then recommend one candidate.\n\nFor existing codebase missions without architecture changes, document the current architecture and explain how the planned changes fit. Scored candidates are not required.\n\n| Candidate | Separation of concerns | Testability | Task independence | Module depth | Total | Recommendation |\n|-----------|------------------------|-------------|-------------------|--------------|-------|----------------|\n| Candidate A | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-12 | Recommended / Not recommended |\n| Candidate B | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-3 | 0-12 | Recommended / Not recommended |\n\n### Scoring Rubric\n\n#### Separation of Concerns\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 3 | Each responsibility maps to exactly one unit. No unit mixes domain logic with transport, storage, or configuration. |\n| 2 | Mostly isolated but 1-2 units mix concerns pragmatically. |\n| 1 | Several units mix concerns. Domain logic tangled with transport or storage in more than one place. |\n| 0 | No clear separation. Business logic, I/O, and configuration interleaved throughout. |\n\n#### Testability\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 3 | Each module testable in isolation with swappable dependencies via interfaces or dependency injection. |\n| 2 | Most modules testable in isolation. One or two require a real external dependency. |\n| 1 | Tests require significant setup or multiple running services. Modules tightly coupled to dependencies. |\n| 0 | No practical way to test modules in isolation. Testing requires the full system running. |\n\n#### Task Independence\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 3 | Separately buildable or deployable units. A task can modify one unit without touching others. |\n| 2 | Explicit interface boundaries within a single build unit with enforced contracts. |\n| 1 | Directory/namespace separation only. No enforcement beyond developer discipline. |\n| 0 | Flat structure. No meaningful boundaries. Every task touches shared state. |\n\n#### Module Depth\n\n| Score | Criteria |\n|-------|----------|\n| 3 | Modules expose small public API relative to implementation. Callers don't need to understand internals. |\n| 2 | Reasonable interface-to-implementation ratio. One or two modules expose more surface than they hide. |\n| 1 | Several modules are thin wrappers. Public API nearly as large as implementation. |\n| 0 | No meaningful abstraction. Public surface and implementation effectively the same. |\n\nAfter the table, include a short rationale for the recommended architecture. If candidates tie on total score, present the tied candidates and their tradeoffs for human selection.\n\n## Module Inventory\n\nList each planned module or major component. Use this table format:\n\n| Module name | Interface width | What it hides | Why this boundary |\n|-------------|-----------------|---------------|-------------------|\n| `<module>` | Narrow / moderate / broad, with the public entry points named | Implementation details callers do not need to know | Reason this responsibility belongs behind this boundary |\n\n## Type Catalog\n\nList domain types only. Type names must come from `UBIQUITOUS_LANGUAGE.md` when the term exists there. Do not include field-level detail.\n\n| Type name | Purpose | Owning module |\n|-----------|---------|---------------|\n| `<type>` | One-line purpose | `<module>` |\n\n## Error Strategy\n\nDefine:\n- Error type hierarchy\n- Propagation rules\n- Which layer throws each error category\n- Which layer catches and translates each error category\n\nKeep this at the design level. Do not list every possible error instance unless the specs require it.\n\n## Integration Points\n\nDescribe how modules call each other. Use this table format:\n\n| Caller | Callee | Method/interface | Direction |\n|--------|--------|------------------|-----------|\n| `<module>` | `<module>` | Function, command, event, adapter, or interface name | Inbound, outbound, or internal |\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"tool_end","dispatch_id":"01KQBBNJ223CRWTGBXX62QBMP9","ts":"2026-04-29T00:55:40Z","tool":"command_execution"}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '165,205p' skills/mission/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"command_run","dispatch_id":"01KQBBNJ223CRWTGBXX62QBMP9","ts":"2026-04-29T00:55:40Z","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '165,205p' skills/mission/SKILL.md\""}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '165,205p' skills/mission/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"### Phase 4.3: Generate and Review Design\n\nGenerate `design.md` (how -- architecture decisions, alternatives considered, risks). Reference the proposal for motivation and specs for requirements.\n\nDesign is required for every mission. The build loop and worker briefs depend on design.md existing -- skipping it would break downstream phases.\n\nFollow the required structure in `templates/design-template.md`. The design must include these five section headings: Architecture Decision, Module Inventory, Type Catalog, Error Strategy, and Integration Points.\n\n#### Module Depth\n\nFor each major component or module in the design, consider its depth. A deep module has a small interface hiding significant implementation. A shallow module has a large interface with thin logic behind it. Prefer deep modules -- they are more testable at the boundary, easier for workers to implement in a single task, and easier for subsequent workers to use correctly.\n\nFor each module, describe:\n- **Interface** -- what callers see (public API, CLI flags, function signatures)\n- **What it hides** -- the complexity behind the interface that callers don't need to know about\n- **Why this boundary** -- what makes this the right place to draw the line\n\nIf a module has a large interface relative to its implementation, ask whether it should be split differently or whether some of its interface belongs in a higher-level module. A good test: can a worker implement this module in a single task and a validator test it at the boundary? If not, the module may be too shallow or too broad.\n\n#### Existing Codebases\n\nWhen the mission modifies an existing codebase (not greenfield), the design must evaluate the current code structure against the planned architecture. For each module the design touches, read the existing code and assess whether it aligns with the target design. If existing modules are shallow, tightly coupled, or conflict with the planned architecture, the design should call this out explicitly and recommend refactoring as part of the implementation. These refactoring decisions flow naturally into task ordering in Phase 4.4 -- refactoring tasks go before the feature tasks that depend on them.\n\nPresent it to the human with a recommendation.\n\nBefore presenting it, run the Phase 4.3 design gate:\n- Verify `design.md` contains all five required section headings from `templates/design-template.md`: Architecture Decision, Module Inventory, Type Catalog, Error Strategy, and Integration Points. If any are missing, regenerate `design.md` before human review.\n- For greenfield missions or missions with explicit architecture changes, verify the Architecture Decision section contains 2-3 scored architecture candidates using the template rubric.\n- For existing codebase missions without architecture changes, scoring is not required. Verify the Architecture Decision section documents the current architecture and explains how the planned changes fit.\n- Enforce the hard floor: no recommended candidate can score 0 on any scoring dimension. If a candidate scores 0 on any dimension, flag it and recommend against it.\n- If candidates tie on total score, present the tied candidates to the human for discussion and selection. Do not apply an arbitrary tie-breaker.\n\nIf approved, proceed to Phase 4.4.\n\n### Phase 4.4: Generate and Review Tasks\n\nGenerate `tasks.md` (ordered checklist of implementation tasks).\n\n#### Task Quality Standard\n\nEach task must be a **vertical slice**: a thin end-to-end path through all layers (schema, API, UI, tests) that delivers narrow but complete functionality.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"tool_end","dispatch_id":"01KQBBNJ223CRWTGBXX62QBMP9","ts":"2026-04-29T00:55:40Z","tool":"command_execution"}
